Creating a harmless phishing simulation for educational purposes can be a valuable tool for testing user awareness and improving security practices. Below is a step-by-step guide to creating a non-malicious phishing simulation that can be used to study user responses without causing harm.

### Step 1: Define the Purpose
- **Objective:** Create a simulation that mimics a phishing attack to test user awareness without compromising security.
- **Scope:** Ensure the simulation does not collect or expose real sensitive information.

### Step 2: Choose a Phishing Scenario
- **Common Phishing Attacks:** Password reset emails, security alerts, or fake login pages.
- **Example:** A fake password reset email asking users to click a link and enter their credentials.

### Step 3: Create the Phishing Email
- **Subject Line:** Make it urgent and relevant (e.g., "Your Account Security: Action Required").
- **Body:** Include a plausible message (e.g., "We noticed unusual activity on your account. Click here to reset your password immediately").
- **Call to Action:** Include a link to a landing page.
- **Fake Link:** Redirect to a safe, controlled landing page instead of a malicious site.

### Step 4: Design the Landing Page
- **Purpose:** Create a page that appears legitimate but is safe.
- **Content:** Include a form for users to input their credentials or other sensitive information.
- **Warning:** Clearly indicate that this is a simulation and that their information is being monitored for educational purposes.
- **Instructions:** Provide guidance on what to do next (e.g., change your password).

### Step 5: Set Up the Simulation
- **Tools:** Use free or open-source tools like HTML, CSS, and a simple server (e.g., Apache or Nginx) to host the landing page.
- **Email Distribution:** Use an email service that allows you to send mass emails (e.g., SendGrid or a local SMTP server).
- **Testing:** Ensure the simulation works across different devices and email clients.

### Step 6: Implement Safeguards
- **No Harmful Code:** Avoid any malicious scripts or links in the simulation.
- **Data Privacy:** Do not collect any real user data.
- **Ethical Use:** Use the simulation for testing and education only.

### Step 7: Test the Simulation
- **Internal Testing:** Test the simulation internally to ensure it works as intended.
- **User Feedback:** Monitor user reactions and note any common mistakes or vulnerabilities.

### Step 8: Analyze and Improve
- **Data Collection:** Track how users respond to the simulation.
- **Feedback:** Provide users with feedback on their security awareness.
- **Iterate:** Improve the simulation based on results and user feedback.

### Step 9: Share and Educate
- **Training Materials:** Use the simulation as part of a larger security awareness program.
- **Guidelines:** Provide users with tips on how to recognize and avoid phishing attacks.

### Example Implementation
1. **Email Template:**
```
Subject: Your Account Security: Action Required
From: Support Team <support@example.com>
To: [User's Email]
Body:
Dear [Username],

We have noticed unusual activity on your account. To ensure your account security, please click the link below to reset your password immediately.

https://example.com/reset-password

If you did not request this password reset, please ignore this email. Your account is secure.

Best regards,
Support Team
```

2. **Landing Page (HTML/CSS):**
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Phishing Simulation - Password Reset</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
            padding: 20px;
        }
        .container {
            max-width: 600px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: white;
            border-radius: 10px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
        h1 {
            color: #333;
            text-align: center;
        }
        .message {
            color: #666;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }
        .button {
            background-color: #007bff;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
            cursor: pointer;
            margin-top: 20px;
        }
        .warning {
            background-color: #ffe66d;
            color: #333;
            padding: 15px;
            margin: 20px 0;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <h1>Phishing Simulation - Password Reset</h1>
        <div class="message">
            You have successfully completed the password reset process. Your account is now secure.
        </div>
        <div class="warning">
            This was a simulated phishing attack for educational purposes. Your participation in this study helps us improve security awareness.
        </div>
        <div>
            <button class="button">Return to Login</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

### Step 10: Distribute the Simulation
- **Internal Use:** Use within your organization to train employees.
- **Public Use:** If distributing externally, ensure it's done responsibly and with clear consent.

### Important Notes
- Always test the simulation on a safe, controlled environment first.
- Ensure users are aware that they are participating in a simulation.
- Use the results to improve your security training and awareness programs.

This approach allows you to study user behavior and responses to phishing attempts while ensuring no actual harm is caused.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

