Creating a phishing simulation program can be a valuable educational tool for users to learn how to recognize and respond to phishing attacks. However, it's important to ensure that the simulation is used responsibly and only for legitimate educational purposes. Below is a basic example of a phishing simulation that you can use to study user responses. This simulation is designed to be harmless and should only be used in a controlled environment.

### Phishing Simulation Example

#### HTML, CSS, and JavaScript Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Phishing Simulation</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f5f5f5;
        }
        .container {
            max-width: 400px;
            margin: 100px auto;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: #fff;
            border-radius: 5px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
        .phishing-page {
            text-align: center;
        }
        .logo {
            width: 200px;
            height: 50px;
            margin-bottom: 30px;
            background-color: #007bff;
            border-radius: 3px;
        }
        .message {
            color: #333;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }
        .form-group {
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }
        input {
            width: 80%;
            padding: 10px;
            margin-top: 5px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 4px;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }
        button {
            background-color: #007bff;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 4px;
            cursor: pointer;
            width: 80%;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #0056b3;
        }
        .timer {
            margin-top: 20px;
            font-weight: bold;
            color: red;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <div class="phishing-page">
            <img src="https://via.placeholder.com/200x50" alt="Logo" class="logo">
            <h2>Welcome to Secure Account</h2>
            <div class="message">
                Your account has been temporarily locked. Please enter your password to unlock your account.
            </div>
            <form id="login-form">
                <div class="form-group">
                    <input type="password" placeholder="Enter your password" required>
                </div>
                <button type="submit">Unlock Account</button>
            </form>
            <div class="timer">This simulation will expire in: <span id="timer">30</span> seconds</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <script>
        // Set a random time limit for the simulation
        let timer = 30;
        let timerInterval = setInterval(function() {
            document.getElementById('timer').textContent = timer;
            timer--;
            if (timer <= 0) {
                clearInterval(timerInterval);
                window.location.href = 'https://www.example.com/safe-page';
            }
        }, 1000);

        // Prevent the page from being closed by the user
        window.onbeforeunload = function() {
            return confirm('Are you sure you want to leave this page?');
        };
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation

1. **HTML Structure**: The simulation includes a simple login page with a logo, a message, a password field, and a submit button. It also includes a timer to add urgency.

2. **CSS Styling**: The page is styled to look like a genuine login page, including fonts, colors, and layout that mimic real-world phishing pages.

3. **JavaScript Functionality**:
   - A countdown timer that redirects the user after 30 seconds.
   - A confirmation dialog when attempting to leave the page.
   - A redirect to a safe page after the timer expires.

### Usage Instructions

1. **Save the code as an HTML file** on your local computer.
2. **Open it in a web browser** to view the simulation.
3. **Test the simulation** by trying to enter different passwords or clicking the "Unlock Account" button.

### Safety Notes

- **Local Testing Only**: This simulation should only be used on your local network or device to ensure it does not attempt to access any external systems.
- **No Harmful Code**: The simulation does not contain any malicious code and is designed to be educational.
- **User Awareness**: Use this tool to educate users about phishing tactics and best practices for online security.

### Ethical Considerations

- Always ensure that phishing simulations are used responsibly and with the consent of the users involved.
- Use this tool to improve awareness and security practices, not to harm or deceive anyone.

By using this simulation, users can learn to recognize the signs of a phishing attack and respond appropriately. For a more advanced simulation, you can add features like:
- Multiple phishing scenarios
- Email-based phishing
- Social engineering elements
- File uploads

However, always ensure that these simulations are used in a controlled environment and for educational purposes only.<｜end▁of▁sentence｜>

