Creating a phishing simulation program can be a useful tool for educational or research purposes, but it must be handled with care to ensure it doesn't cause harm or violate ethical standards. Phishing simulations are often used to educate users about security best practices and help them recognize potential threats.

Here's an example of a simple, harmless phishing simulation that you can create. This simulation is designed to look like a legitimate email or website but is clearly marked as a test for educational purposes.

### Phishing Simulation Example (Harmless)

#### HTML, CSS, and JavaScript Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Phishing Simulation</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
            margin: 0;
            padding: 20px;
        }
        .container {
            max-width: 800px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 20px;
            background-color: white;
            border-radius: 5px;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
        }
        .email-content {
            padding: 20px;
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            border-radius: 5px;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }
        .button {
            background-color: #007bff;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 20px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 4px;
            cursor: pointer;
            font-size: 16px;
            margin: 10px 0;
        }
        .button:hover {
            background-color: #0056b3;
